AB  - The integration of Large Language Model (LLM) into remote healthcare support systems offers significant opportunities but also introduces critical safety and accountability challenges. This paper presents a safety-oriented architecture for LLM-assisted health guidance that enforces deterministic risk triage and guideline grounding before any language generation occurs. Patient messages are classified into four safety-critical categories: emergency, requiring immediate medical attention; clinician escalation, indicating higher-risk contexts requiring professional evaluation; self-care, appropriate for general guideline-based advice; and refuse, covering requests such as diagnosis or medication dosing that cannot be safely addressed. The proposed architecture separates processing into modular components, including rule-based triage, retrieval-augmented guideline matching, constrained response generation, and immutable audit logging. Emergency and Refusal routes are handled deterministically without any language model involvement, while self-care and escalation cases receive evidence-grounded responses with explicit citations and safety disclaimers. A working prototype was implemented using FastAPI, Term Frequency-Inverse Document Frequency (TF-IDF) retrieval over curated guidelines, and optional LLM integration. An automated scenario-based evaluation framework was developed to assess system performance. Experimental results on synthetic patient cases achieved 98% routing accuracy, 100% emergency recall, 100% refusal precision, and 92.3% citation coverage, demonstrating reliable classification and grounded response generation. The proposed design illustrates how LLM-enabled healthcare systems can be structured to improve safety, transparency, and regulatory readiness.
ER  - 


