AB  - This research introduces a knowledge-enhanced generative method for preliminary residential layout design with Graph Topological Constraints using large language models (LLMs), addressing the intricate requirements and expertise needed in architectural design. Leveraging the RPLAN dataset of over 80,000 real residential layouts, the study transforms layout data into graph-structured formats and develops a specialized knowledge base, encapsulating essential elements such as room types, spatial relationships, area metrics, and adjacency matrices. This enhancement improves the LLMs' capability to generate high-quality, adaptable preliminary designs that meet complex spatial and functional constraints. The generated layouts undergo a comprehensive evaluation using Structural Similarity Index (SSIM), Jaccard similarity, and Peak Signal-to-Noise Ratio (PSNR), complemented by Principal Component Analysis (PCA) to assess real-world data alignment and a professional building standards score assessment. Results show that the knowledge-enhanced LLM method surpasses traditional fine-tuning approaches, producing layouts with strong structural coherence and spatial accuracy while showing potential for scaling to more complex designs. This study provides a foundational approach for integrating LLMs into architectural layout generation, presenting a robust and efficient tool to streamline the architectural design process.
ER  - 


